 "youtube": {
  "title": "Fork History: Why Was Eating With One a Sin?",
  "description": "Fork history starts with a scandal. In 1004 a Byzantine princess married the son of the doge of Venice, and at the table she refused to touch her food with her fingers: her servants cut it up and she ate with a small two-pronged golden fork. To many in Venice it looked like vanity. When plague killed her, her husband and their son a few years later, the churchman Peter Damian presented her death as God's punishment. Six centuries on, in 1611, the English traveller Thomas Coryat was still mocked as a \"fork-bearer\" for praising forks.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for the strange origins of everyday things. Which ordinary object do you think has the strangest history? Tell us in the comments!\n\n#shorts #historyofthefork #stickstory",
